{"parents": [{"link": "../", "title": "Frequently Asked Questions"}], "prev": {"link": "../how-to-use-webhooks/", "title": "How to use webhooks?"}, "next": {"link": "../using-multiple-clients-at-once-on-the-same-account/", "title": "Using multiple clients at once on the same account"}, "title": "Using the same file_id across different accounts", "meta": null, "body": "<section id=\"using-the-same-file-id-across-different-accounts\">\n<h1>Using the same file_id across different accounts<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#using-the-same-file-id-across-different-accounts\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1>\n<p>Telegram file_id strings are bound to the account which generated them. An attempt in using a foreign file id will\nresult in errors such as <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">[400</span> <span class=\"pre\">MEDIA_EMPTY]</span></code>. The only exception are stickers\u2019 file ids; you can use them across\ndifferent accounts without any problem.</p>\n</section>\n", "metatags": "<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\" />\n", "rellinks": [["genindex", "General Index", "I", "index"], ["py-modindex", "Python Module Index", "", "modules"], ["faq/using-multiple-clients-at-once-on-the-same-account", "Using multiple clients at once on the same account", "N", "next"], ["faq/how-to-use-webhooks", "How to use webhooks?", "P", "previous"]], "sourcename": "", "toc": "<ul>\n<li><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#\">Using the same file_id across different accounts</a></li>\n</ul>\n", "display_toc": false, "page_source_suffix": ".rst", "current_page_name": "faq/using-the-same-file-id-across-different-accounts", "sidebars": ["sidebar/brand.html", "sidebar/search.html", "sidebar/scroll-start.html", "sidebar/navigation.html", "sidebar/scroll-end.html"], "alabaster_version": "1.0.0", "alabaster_version_info": [1, 0, 0], "furo_version": "2025.12.19", "furo_navigation_tree": "", "furo_hide_toc": true, "furo_pygments": {"light": {"background": "#eeffcc", "foreground": "black"}, "dark": {"background": "#202020", "foreground": "#d0d0d0"}}}